# Translations vs mocks vs server

## Translations (`dictionaries/`)

UI chrome only: nav, page chrome, auth, player controls, shared labels.

These stay in the client bundle (or static messages). Do **not** put catalog/CMS fields here.

## Mocks (`mocks/` + structural records in `lib/`)

Temporary stand-ins for **server-backed catalog data**. Assume they will be replaced by API/CMS responses (often already localized for the request locale).

Includes:

- `mocks/content/{he,en}.json` — demo localized catalog copy
- `mocks/name-bank.ts` — seed pools for new demo rows
- `lib/routines.ts` / `lib/instructors.ts` — structural demo records (slugs, keys, media, pricing)

Rules:

- Do not treat bank names or song/artist pairs as final/legal content
- Instagram URLs may be placeholders — replace only with URLs the user supplies
- Prefer `mocks/name-bank.ts` for new demo rows
- Resolve mock catalog copy via `getMockContent` / `lib/i18n/localize.ts`, not UI dictionaries
- When adding features, keep a clear boundary: UI strings in `dictionaries/`, catalog entities fetched (today: mocked) through catalog helpers — do not hard-wire demo rows deeper into components than necessary

## Will come from the server (do not bake as permanent client data)

- Routines / tutorials (metadata, media, chapters, pricing, technique, descriptions)
- Teachers / instructors (name, bio, role/style, avatar, Instagram)
- Catalog taxonomy labels when they are content (styles, levels, tags) for a locale
- User-specific data (purchases, account library, auth profile beyond Firebase client session)

Prefer `lib/server/catalog` (and `/api/v1/*`) for new catalog reads. When the API exists with a durable store, prefer server-provided locale fields for catalog copy and keep `dictionaries/` for product UI chrome only.
